- `action(n1, "start selling cars")`: Payload adds "start", diverging from the source text "sell cars".
- `branch(b3, AND)`: Enforces concurrent execution of bonus and paycheck checks, whereas the source "If you 'get a bonus'... If you 'get a paycheck'..." describes conditional continuations, not parallel mandatory steps.
- `precedence(b3, n2)` & `precedence(b3, n3)` (missing converge): Creates two disjoint paths that never rejoin, contradicting the source's single linear progression where either condition leads to continuing and subsequently paying the bill.
- `action(n8, "end process")`, `action(n9, "end process")`, `end(e1)`, `end(e2)`: Hallucinates duplicate termination actions and two separate end nodes instead of a single transition directly after paying the bill as stated in the source.
- `defines(v1, "get a bonus", {"no"})` & `defines(v2, "get a paycheck", {"no"})`: Restricts variable domains to `{"no"}`, omitting positive values necessary to evaluate "If you 'get a bonus'" and "If you 'get a paycheck'".
- `guard(b1, n4, v1 = 'no')` & `guard(b2, n5, v2 = 'no')`: Requires variables to equal `'no'` to proceed, inverting the source logic where receiving a bonus or paycheck triggers continuation.
- `branch(b1, XOR)` & `branch(b2, XOR)`: Introduces redundant split nodes after `n2` and `n3` despite having only single outgoing edges, unnecessarily fragmenting the direct conditional flow described.
- `label(b3, n2, "no")` & `label(b3, n3, "no")`: Assigns unsupported "no" labels to edges initiating the checks, which has no basis in the source and conflicts with the intended conditional semantics.

Structural Check
- [S1] STC9 failed: Each branch node must have exactly one incoming and at least two outgoing edges. | Node `b1` has in=1 and out=1. | Node `b2` has in=1 and out=1. | DSL: branch(b1,XOR); branch(b2,XOR)
- [S2] STC11 failed: Each variable definition must have a valid range with at least two distinct values. | Variable `v1` has an invalid range `['no']`. | Variable `v2` has an invalid range `['no']`.
- [S3] STC15 failed: Every variable used in a guard must be formally defined. | Guard `v1 = 'no'` on `b1 -> n4` uses undefined variable `v1`. | Guard `v2 = 'no'` on `b2 -> n5` uses undefined variable `v2`. | DSL: guard(b1,n4,"v1 = 'no'"); guard(b2,n5,"v2 = 'no'")

Logical Sanity Check
- [L1] LSC1 failed: Every guard must be satisfiable under the declared ranges of its referenced variables. | Guard `v1 = 'no'` on `b1 -> n4` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | Guard `v2 = 'no'` on `b2 -> n5` cannot be evaluated: `v2` has no declared range. | DSL: guard(b1,n4,"v1 = 'no'"); guard(b2,n5,"v2 = 'no'")
- [L2] LSC3 failed: Guards outgoing from XOR/OR branches must cover the whole evaluated state space. | Branch `b1` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | Branch `b2` cannot be evaluated: `v2` has no declared range.
